## The decisions, area by area

### 1. The command line and input

What every tool shares: how its arguments split into options and files, and how it reads its input and splits it into lines.

- **[SW-261126-ZG5V](cli.md#sw-261126-zg5v-a-tool-reads-standard-input-or-the-named-files-in-order-split-into-lines-a-last-unterminated-one-included)** A tool reads standard input or the named files in order, split into lines, a last unterminated one included *(structure)*  
  Why: GNU treats a missing final newline as a line that ends there, and so does every script that pipes a file made by printf; the tools share one reader so they cannot differ on it.
- **[SW-261126-2QDT](cli.md#sw-261126-2qdt-options-are-parsed-as-gnu-parses-them-and-one-a-tool-does-not-know-is-refused)** Options are parsed as GNU parses them, and one a tool does not know is refused  
  Why: scripts write options every way getopt accepts, and a parser that took fewer would turn a working command line into an error or, worse, read an option as a file.

### 2. wc

What wc counts in an input, which counts it prints, and how it lays them out.

- **[SW-261126-4W6F](wc.md#sw-261126-4w6f-wc-counts-newlines-words-of-printable-bytes-and-bytes)** wc counts newlines, words of printable bytes, and bytes *(invariant)*  
  Why: this is GNU 9.4's count in the C locale, and a script that counts records with wc -l relies on the newline rule exactly.
- **[SW-261126-MKWM](wc.md#sw-261126-mkwm-wc-prints-the-counts--l--w-and--c-select-all-three-when-none-is-always-in-that-order)** wc prints the counts -l, -w and -c select, all three when none is, always in that order  
  Why: GNU's order is fixed, and a script that reads the second column as bytes after `wc -cl` relies on it.
- **[SW-261126-SJ3N](wc.md#sw-261126-sj3n-wc-right-aligns-every-count-to-one-width-and-prints-the-files-name-after)** wc right-aligns every count to one width and prints the file's name after  
  Why: this is GNU's layout; `wc -l < f` printing a bare number is what every script depends on, and the padded form is what people's eyes and awk's fields are used to.

### 3. cut

How cut -f selects fields from each line, what it does with a line that has no delimiter, and which options it takes.

- **[SW-261126-972N](cut.md#sw-261126-972n-cut--f-prints-the-listed-fields-of-each-line-once-each-in-the-order-they-stand)** cut -f prints the listed fields of each line, once each, in the order they stand  
  Why: GNU selects a set, not a sequence: -f3,1 is -f1,3, and a script that wanted to reorder columns uses awk.
- **[SW-261126-7RFJ](cut.md#sw-261126-7rfj-a-line-without-the-delimiter-is-printed-whole)** A line without the delimiter is printed whole *(invariant)*  
  Why: this is GNU's default, and it is what keeps header lines, comments and blank lines in a script's output; GNU's -s is how a script asks to drop them.
- **[SW-261126-B85M](cut.md#sw-261126-b85m-cut-takes-a-field-list-it-must-be-given-and-a-one-byte-delimiter-a-tab-when-none-is-given)** cut takes a field list it must be given and a one-byte delimiter, a tab when none is given  
  Why: GNU's defaults and refusals; a cut that guessed a delimiter or a list would print columns nobody chose.

### 4. sort

How sort orders the lines of its input: by bytes, by number with -n, and in reverse with -r.

- **[SW-261126-YMCY](sort.md#sw-261126-ymcy-sort-orders-whole-lines-by-their-bytes-and--r-reverses-the-order)** sort orders whole lines by their bytes, and -r reverses the order *(invariant)*  
  Why: in the C locale GNU's order is the bytes' order; scripts rely on it being the same on every machine, which is why they set LC_ALL=C.
- **[SW-261126-GKC7](sort.md#sw-261126-gkc7-sort--n-orders-by-the-leading-number-exactly-and-breaks-ties-by-bytes)** sort -n orders by the leading number, exactly, and breaks ties by bytes  
  Why: GNU's -n is a string comparison of decimal numbers, not a float parse, so huge numbers and 2.5 against 2.50 order the same on every machine; the byte tie-break makes the output one order, not one of several.

### 5. uniq

How uniq merges runs of repeated lines, and how -c counts them.

- **[SW-261126-4T6M](uniq.md#sw-261126-4t6m-uniq-writes-each-run-of-adjacent-byte-identical-lines-once)** uniq writes each run of adjacent byte-identical lines once *(invariant)*  
  Why: GNU's uniq is a filter over adjacent lines, which is why scripts put sort in front of it; and case matters in the C locale, so "Apple" and "apple" are two records.
- **[SW-261126-8785](uniq.md#sw-261126-8785-uniq--c-prefixes-each-line-with-its-runs-length-in-seven-columns-and-a-space)** uniq -c prefixes each line with its run's length in seven columns and a space  
  Why: GNU's format; `sort | uniq -c | sort -n` is among the most common pipelines there are, and it relies on the count leading the line.
// Command sort prints the lines of its input in order, as GNU sort does in
// the C locale: by bytes, or by number with -n, reversed with -r.
package main

import (
	"bytes"
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"os"
	"slices"

	"example.com/coreutils/internal/input"
	"example.com/coreutils/internal/opts"
)

func main() {
	os.Exit(run(os.Args[1:], os.Stdin, os.Stdout, os.Stderr))
}

var spec = opts.Spec{
	Flags: "nr",
	Long:  map[string]byte{"numeric-sort": 'n', "reverse": 'r'},
}

// order is how lines compare: by number or by bytes, and whether reversed.
type order struct {
	numeric, reverse bool
}

// compare orders two lines. By bytes, the lines compare as unsigned byte
// strings. By number, the leading numbers compare and lines whose numbers
// are equal fall back to the byte comparison. Reversal reverses the whole
// comparison, the fallback with it.
//
//charter:realizes SW-261126-YMCY, SW-261126-GKC7
func (o order) compare(a, b []byte) int {
	c := 0
	if o.numeric {
		c = numcompare(a, b)
	}
	if c == 0 {
		c = bytes.Compare(a, b)
	}
	if o.reverse {
		c = -c
	}
	return c
}

// number is a line's leading number, as -n reads it: its sign, its
// integer digits without leading zeros and its fraction digits without
// trailing zeros.
type number struct {
	neg      bool
	whole    []byte
	fraction []byte
}

// parseNumber reads a line's leading number: blanks, an optional minus,
// digits, and an optional point with more digits. A line that does not
// start with one reads as zero, and so does negative zero.
//
//charter:realizes SW-261126-GKC7
func parseNumber(s []byte) number {
	i := 0
	for i < len(s) && (s[i] == ' ' || s[i] == '\t') {
		i++
	}
	var n number
	if i < len(s) && s[i] == '-' {
		n.neg = true
		i++
	}
	start := i
	for i < len(s) && s[i] >= '0' && s[i] <= '9' {
		i++
	}
	n.whole = bytes.TrimLeft(s[start:i], "0")
	if i < len(s) && s[i] == '.' {
		i++
		start = i
		for i < len(s) && s[i] >= '0' && s[i] <= '9' {
			i++
		}
		n.fraction = bytes.TrimRight(s[start:i], "0")
	}
	if len(n.whole) == 0 && len(n.fraction) == 0 {
		n.neg = false
	}
	return n
}

// numcompare compares the leading numbers of two lines exactly, digit by
// digit, however long they are.
func numcompare(a, b []byte) int {
	x, y := parseNumber(a), parseNumber(b)
	if x.neg != y.neg {
		if x.neg {
			return -1
		}
		return 1
	}
	c := magnitude(x, y)
	if x.neg {
		c = -c
	}
	return c
}

func magnitude(x, y number) int {
	if len(x.whole) != len(y.whole) {
		if len(x.whole) < len(y.whole) {
			return -1
		}
		return 1
	}
	if c := bytes.Compare(x.whole, y.whole); c != 0 {
		return c
	}
	return bytes.Compare(x.fraction, y.fraction)
}

func run(args []string, stdin io.Reader, stdout, stderr io.Writer) int {
	given, names, err := opts.Parse(args, spec)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Fprintf(stderr, "sort: %v\nTry 'sort --help' for more information.\n", err)
		return 2
	}
	var o order
	for _, g := range given {
		switch g.Name {
		case 'n':
			o.numeric = true
		case 'r':
			o.reverse = true
		}
	}
	srcs, err := input.Read(names, stdin)
	if err != nil {
		fmt.Fprintf(stderr, "sort: %v\n", err)
		return 2
	}
	var lines [][]byte
	for _, s := range srcs {
		lines = append(lines, input.Lines(s.Data)...)
	}
	slices.SortStableFunc(lines, o.compare)
	for _, l := range lines {
		stdout.Write(append(l, '\n'))
	}
	return 0
}
package main

import (
	"bytes"
	"strings"
	"testing"

	"example.com/coreutils/claims"
	"github.com/leroux/charter/chartertest"
)

// sorted runs the command on lines given on standard input and returns
// the lines it printed, failing the test unless it exits 0.
func sorted(t *testing.T, stdin string, args ...string) string {
	t.Helper()
	var out, errOut bytes.Buffer
	if code := run(args, strings.NewReader(stdin), &out, &errOut); code != 0 {
		t.Fatalf("sort %q: exit %d: %s", args, code, errOut.String())
	}
	return out.String()
}

func TestSortKeepsAnUnterminatedLastLine(t *testing.T) {
	chartertest.Verifies(t, claims.STK_261126_3NDQ, "last-line-kept")
	if got := sorted(t, "b\na"); got != "a\nb\n" {
		t.Errorf("sort of \"b\\na\" = %q, want a and b, each with its newline", got)
	}
}

func TestSortRefusesAnUnknownFlag(t *testing.T) {
	chartertest.Verifies(t, claims.STK_261126_WSGT, "unknown-flag-fails")
	var out, errOut bytes.Buffer
	code := run([]string{"-nZ"}, strings.NewReader("2\n1\n"), &out, &errOut)
	if code != 2 || out.Len() != 0 || !strings.Contains(errOut.String(), "'Z'") {
		t.Errorf("sort -nZ: exit %d, stdout %q, stderr %q; want exit 2, no output, Z named", code, out.String(), errOut.String())
	}
}

func TestLinesSortByTheirBytes(t *testing.T) {
	chartertest.Verifies(t, claims.SW_261126_YMCY, "bytewise")
	if got := sorted(t, "b\nB\na\nA\nab\n"); got != "A\nB\na\nab\nb\n" {
		t.Errorf("sort = %q, want A B a ab b", got)
	}
}

func TestReverseReversesTheOrder(t *testing.T) {
	chartertest.Verifies(t, claims.SW_261126_YMCY, "reverse")
	if got := sorted(t, "b\nB\na\nA\nab\n", "-r"); got != "b\nab\na\nB\nA\n" {
		t.Errorf("sort -r = %q, want b ab a B A", got)
	}
}

func TestNumbersSortByValue(t *testing.T) {
	chartertest.Verifies(t, claims.SW_261126_GKC7, "numeric-order")
	if got := sorted(t, "10\n9\n-1\n2.5\n", "-n"); got != "-1\n2.5\n9\n10\n" {
		t.Errorf("sort -n = %q, want -1 2.5 9 10", got)
	}
}

func TestANonNumberReadsAsZero(t *testing.T) {
	chartertest.Verifies(t, claims.SW_261126_GKC7, "non-number-zero")
	// The zeros tie and fall back to bytes: "+5" < "-0" < "abc".
	if got := sorted(t, "1\nabc\n-1\n+5\n-0\n", "-n"); got != "-1\n+5\n-0\nabc\n1\n" {
		t.Errorf("sort -n = %q, want -1, the zeros by bytes, 1", got)
	}
}

func TestEqualNumbersTieByBytes(t *testing.T) {
	chartertest.Verifies(t, claims.SW_261126_GKC7, "ties-bytewise")
	if got := sorted(t, "2.50\n2.5\n", "-n"); got != "2.5\n2.50\n" {
		t.Errorf("sort -n = %q, want 2.5 then 2.50", got)
	}
	if got := sorted(t, "2.5\n2.50\n", "-rn"); got != "2.50\n2.5\n" {
		t.Errorf("sort -rn = %q, want 2.50 then 2.5", got)
	}
}

func TestNumbersCompareExactly(t *testing.T) {
	chartertest.Verifies(t, claims.SW_261126_GKC7, "exact-digits")
	in := "99999999999999999999\n99999999999999999998\n-99999999999999999998\n-99999999999999999999\n"
	want := "-99999999999999999999\n-99999999999999999998\n99999999999999999998\n99999999999999999999\n"
	if got := sorted(t, in, "-n"); got != want {
		t.Errorf("sort -n = %q, want %q", got, want)
	}
}

